        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Legal Eligibility"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
          },
          "code": "CANDIDATE_LEGAL_ELIGIBILITY",
          "id": "1769031892452",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769031892455_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (Candidate Metadata Subset)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query, extract explicitly mentioned candidate metadata attributes, and structure them into the Output JSON format defined below. Follow these rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt and preserves all behavioral constraints, redundancy, and enforcement logic, but applies ONLY to the fields listed in the Output JSON Format section.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n\nGlobal Include/Exclude Intent Contract (CRITICAL)\n\n- Parse the query into local clauses (split by punctuation and connectors such as and, but, except, other than, excluding, without, not).\n- For each extracted value in this subset, bind intent at clause level as INCLUDE or EXCLUDE.\n- A value with explicit exclusion intent MUST NOT appear in an include field for the same semantic domain.\n- If a value appears in both include and exclude intent, exclusion intent takes precedence for that value.\n- Exclusion intent is clause-scoped; do not leak negation to unrelated clauses unless explicitly global.\n- Keep schema unchanged and populate only fields defined in this subset.\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate a JSON attribute only if it is explicitly mentioned in the query.\n- Do not assume, infer, or guess values.\n- Do not derive values from other fields.\n- Do not add commentary or explanations; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n- Certain fields are conditional or mutually exclusive; respect those constraints strictly.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [],\n  \"Languages\": [],\n  \"Citizenship\": [],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"\",\n  \"Labels\": [],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nField-Specific Extraction Rules\n\nCandidate Type\n\n- Populate Candidate Type only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Do NOT infer Candidate Type from employer, job title, or work history.\n- Do NOT populate Candidate Type when query contains employer, job title, or other filters unless Candidate Type is explicitly stated.\n- Allowed values: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.enabledCandidateTypes}}\n- Only use candidate types that are present in the enabled candidate types list above.\n- If the query uses generic terms like \"candidates,\" leave this field blank.\n\nTrigger Phrases and Mappings (only map to enabled candidate types from the list above):\n- \"internal candidates\" → [\"employee\"] (if \"employee\" is enabled)\n- \"external candidates\" or \"external workers\" → [\"external\"] (if \"external\" is enabled)\n- \"former employees\" or \"alumni\" → [\"ex-employee\"] (if \"ex-employee\" is enabled)\n- \"contract workers\" or \"contractors\" or \"contingent workers\" → [\"contingent\"] (if \"contingent\" is enabled)\n- \"temporary workers\" → [\"contingent\", \"employee\", \"ex-contingent\"] (only include types that are enabled)\n- \"freelancers\" or \"gig workers\" → [\"contingent\"] (if \"contingent\" is enabled)\n- \"candidates\" (generic) → [] (leave blank)\n\nCritical Rule - Mutual Exclusivity with Employer:\n- Do NOT populate Candidate Type when only employer filters are mentioned.\n- Example: \"candidates who have formerly worked at companies like Meta\" → Candidate Type: [] (blank), Former Employer: [synonyms]\n\nExamples:\n- \"Internal candidates\" → [\"employee\"]\n- \"External workers\" → [\"external\"]\n- \"Former employees\" → [\"ex-employee\"]\n- \"Internal candidates with 10 years experience\" → [\"employee\"]\n- \"Candidates from Google\" → [] (do not infer from employer)\n- \"Former employees who work at Google\" → [\"ex-employee\"] (explicitly mentioned \"former employees\")\n- \"Employees who formerly worked at Google\" → [\"employee\"] (explicitly mentioned \"employees\")\n\n---\n\nLicenses And Certifications\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Do not infer certifications from job titles or education.\n- Expand the input with commonly used industry synonyms or equivalent credentials.\n- Do not return more than 5 values.\n\nTrigger Phrases:\nlicense, licensed, certification, certified, credential, accreditation, clearance, professional credentials\n\nSynonym Expansion Examples:\n- \"CPA certified\" → [\"CPA\", \"Certified Public Accountant\", \"Accounting License\"]\n- \"Family Nurse Practitioner license\" → [\"FNP\", \"FNP-BC\", \"Family Nurse Practitioner\", \"Family NP\"]\n- \"Cloud Computing license\" → [\"Cloud Computing\", \"AWS\", \"GCP Architect\", \"OCI Consultant\", \"Azure Certified\"]\n- \"Tax License\" → [\"CPA\", \"CFA\", \"Accounting\", \"Taxation\", \"Tax Professional\"]\n- \"Teaching credentials\" → [\"Teaching License\", \"Teaching Certificate\", \"Education Credential\"]\n- \"CFA certification\" → [\"CFA\", \"Certified Financial Analyst\", \"CFA Charterholder\"]\n\nExamples:\n- \"Teachers with valid teaching credentials\" → [\"Teaching License\", \"Teaching Certificate\", \"Education Credential\"]\n- \"Family Nurse Practitioner certification\" → [\"Family Nurse Practitioner\", \"FNP-BC\", \"FNP\", \"Family NP\"]\n- \"Candidates certified in CFA\" → [\"CFA\", \"Certified Financial Analyst\", \"CFA Charterholder\"]\n\n---\n\nLanguages\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- This is a multi-value field.\n- Do not infer language from location or citizenship.\n\nTrigger Phrases:\nfluent in, speaks, can read, can write, well versed in, conversant in, bilingual, converse in, ability to write in\n\nExamples:\n- \"Fluent in French and Spanish\" → [\"French\", \"Spanish\"]\n- \"Candidates having ability to write in Hindi\" → [\"Hindi\"]\n- \"Bilingual English and Mandarin\" → [\"English\", \"Mandarin\"]\n\n---\n\nCitizenship\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Do NOT infer citizenship from residence or location-related phrases.\n- \"Lives in\" / \"based in\" / \"resides in\" / \"relocated to\" / \"currently in\" → These refer to LOCATION, not citizenship.\n- \"Citizen of\" / \"citizenship\" / \"passport\" / \"national\" / \"nationality\" / \"born\" / \"naturalized\" → These refer to CITIZENSHIP.\n- Populate values using ISO-2 country codes (uppercase).\n\nCritical Rule:\n- Query: \"lives in India\" → Citizenship: [], Location: { \"Country\": \"IN\" }\n- Query: \"citizen of India\" → Citizenship: [\"IN\"]\n\nExamples:\n- \"US citizen\" → [\"US\"]\n- \"Indian national\" → [\"IN\"]\n- \"US nationals\" → [\"US\"]\n- \"Lives in Canada\" → Citizenship: [] (populate Location instead)\n- \"Candidates having citizenship from France\" → [\"FR\"]\n- \"Born in Germany\" → [\"DE\"]\n\n---\n\nCandidate Number\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Represents a system-generated unique identifier.\n- Accept alphanumeric identifiers exactly as provided.\n- Do not infer from names or other identifiers.\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidate number A34556\" → \"A34556\"\n- \"Candidate ID 998877\" → \"998877\"\n- \"Looking for candidate having Id 44545423\" → \"44545423\"\n- \"Candidate fgrt6y rtrtrgr\" → \"fgrt6y rtrtrgr\"\n- \"rtrtrgr\" → \"rtrtrgr\"\n\n---\n\nLabels\n\n\n\nLabel Include Guard (CRITICAL):\n- If label/tag text appears inside an exclusion clause (exclude, excluding, without, not, except, other than), DO NOT add that value to Labels.\n- Populate Labels only for affirmative label/tag clauses.\n- If a query contains both included and excluded labels, put only affirmative values in Labels; excluded values belong only to Labels Excluded (handled in excluded-fields subset).\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates with Java tag but exclude nursing tag\" -> Labels: [\"Java\"]\n- \"Candidates with excluded nursing tag\" -> Labels: []\n- \"Candidates not tagged Java but tagged Python\" -> Labels: [\"Python\"]\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned using explicit label terminology.\n- Do not infer labels from skills, job titles, or employers.\n- When label terminology is used with a skill or job title value, populate ONLY Labels and leave Skills/Job Title blank.\n\nTrigger Phrases:\nlabel, labels, tag, tags, categorized as, grouped as, labeled as\n\nCritical Labels vs Job Title/Skills Rule:\n- When query contains both label terminology AND a job title/skill value:\n  - If \"label\" or \"tag\" is used for the value → populate Labels only\n  - If both label and regular job title are mentioned → populate both appropriately\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates with nursing tags\" → Labels: [\"Nursing\"], Job Title: [], Skills: []\n- \"Software engineer label\" → Labels: [\"Software Engineer\"], Job Title: [], Skills: []\n- \"Candidates with software engineer label\" → Labels: [\"Software Engineer\"], Job Title: [], Skills: []\n- \"Software engineers with software engineer tag\" → Labels: [\"Software Engineer\"], Job Title: [\"Software Engineer\", \"Software Developer\", \"Application Developer\", \"Front-end Engineer\", \"Full Stack Engineer\"]\n- \"Find software engineers with programming skills and categorized as programming experts\" → Labels: [\"Programming Experts\"], Job Title: [\"Software Engineer\", ...], Skills: [\"Java\", \"Python\", \"C++\", ...]\n- \"Candidates with CPA label\" → Labels: [\"CPA\"]\n- \"Candidates with mechanical engineer label\" → Labels: [\"Mechanical Engineer\"]\n\n---\n\nWork Experience Range\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Leave the fields blank unless user specifies a numerical value or range.\n- Represented as an array of two integers: [fromValue, toValue]\n- Single value searches must set both values equal.\n- Ranges must respect greater than / less than semantics.\n- Round decimal years UP to the nearest integer (ceiling).\n- Convert months to years and round DOWN (floor). Example: 6 months → 0, 20 months → 1\n- Never return decimal values.\n\nRules by Query Pattern:\n\n| Query Pattern | Output |\n|---------------|--------|\n| \"7 years experience\" | [7, 7] |\n| \"3-5 years\" | [3, 5] |\n| \"More than 7 years\" or \"at least 7 years\" | [7, 99] |\n| \"Less than 7 years\" or \"at most 7 years\" | [0, 7] |\n| \"6 months\" | [0, 0] |\n| \"3 months\" | [0, 0] |\n| \"20 months\" | [1, 1] |\n| \"2.5 years\" | [3, 3] (round up) |\n\nExamples:\n- \"7 years of exp\" → [7, 7]\n- \"Show me external candidates with 6 months of experience\" → Work Experience Range: [0, 0], Candidate Type: [\"external\"]\n- \"Show me external candidates with 3 months of experience\" → Work Experience Range: [0, 0], Candidate Type: [\"external\"]\n- \"Find me candidates with 5 years of experience in python\" → Work Experience Range: [5, 5], Skills: [\"Python\", \"Python Programming\", \"Django\", \"NumPy\", \"PyTorch\"]\n- \"Find me candidates with 3-5 years of experience in SAP\" → Work Experience Range: [3, 5], Employer: [\"SAP\", \"SAP Inc\"]\n- \"Find me candidates with more than 7 years of experience in SAP\" → Work Experience Range: [7, 99], Employer: [\"SAP\", \"SAP Inc\"]\n- \"Find me candidates with less than 7 years of experience in SAP\" → Work Experience Range: [0, 7], Employer: [\"SAP\", \"SAP Inc\"]\n- \"Find me candidates with atleast 7 years of experience in SAP\" → Work Experience Range: [7, 99], Employer: [\"SAP\", \"SAP Inc\"]\n- \"Find me candidates with atmost 7 years of experience in SAP\" → Work Experience Range: [0, 7], Employer: [\"SAP\", \"SAP Inc\"]\n\nNote: In the SAP examples above, \"SAP\" refers to the employer/company, not a skill.\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nExample 1: Candidate Type with Experience\nQuery: \"Internal candidates with 10 years experience\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [\"employee\"],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [],\n  \"Languages\": [],\n  \"Citizenship\": [],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"\",\n  \"Labels\": [],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": [10, 10]\n}\n\nExample 2: Citizenship and Languages\nQuery: \"Find US nationals fluent in Spanish with 4-6 years of experience\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [],\n  \"Languages\": [\"Spanish\"],\n  \"Citizenship\": [\"US\"],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"\",\n  \"Labels\": [],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": [4, 6]\n}\n\nExample 3: Labels Only\nQuery: \"Candidates with CPA label\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [],\n  \"Languages\": [],\n  \"Citizenship\": [],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"\",\n  \"Labels\": [\"CPA\"],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": []\n}\n\nExample 4: Contract Workers with Certifications\nQuery: \"Contract workers with CPA certification and less than 3 years experience\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [\"contingent\"],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [\"CPA\", \"Certified Public Accountant\", \"Accounting License\"],\n  \"Languages\": [],\n  \"Citizenship\": [],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"\",\n  \"Labels\": [],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": [0, 3]\n}\n\nExample 5: Candidate Number\nQuery: \"Find candidate number A34556\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [],\n  \"Languages\": [],\n  \"Citizenship\": [],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"A34556\",\n  \"Labels\": [],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": []\n}\n\nExample 6: Complex Metadata\nQuery: \"Former employees who are Indian nationals fluent in French and Spanish with nursing tags\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Type\": [\"ex-employee\"],\n  \"Licenses And Certifications\": [],\n  \"Languages\": [\"French\", \"Spanish\"],\n  \"Citizenship\": [\"IN\"],\n  \"Candidate Number\": \"\",\n  \"Labels\": [\"Nursing\"],\n  \"Work Experience Range\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate fields outside this subset.\n- Never infer across fields.\n- Never infer Candidate Type from employer fields alone.\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: \nSearch Text: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Intent Flags"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
          },
          "code": "CANDIDATE_INTENT_FLAGS",
          "id": "1769031919409",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769031919410_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (Boolean & Status Flags Subset)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query and extract explicitly mentioned boolean and status-based attributes related to candidate state, preferences, and eligibility. You must structure the extracted data into the Output JSON format defined below and follow all rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt and preserves the same behavioral constraints, enforcement rules, redundancy, and strictness. It applies ONLY to the fields listed in the Output JSON Format section.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate a field ONLY if it is explicitly mentioned in the query.\n- Do NOT assume, infer, or guess values.\n- Do NOT default boolean values to false.\n- If a boolean condition is not explicitly stated, leave the field blank (empty string).\n- Do not add commentary or explanation; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n- These fields are independent unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\n\n---\n\nBoolean and Status Field Extraction Rules\n\nHas Active Job Applications\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n- Leave blank (empty string) unless the query clearly specifies application activity or status.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- active job applications\n- active application\n- currently in an application process\n- applications still in progress\n- applied and not rejected\n- has applied to open requisitions\n- applicants who have not yet been hired or rejected\n- active applicants\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates with active job applications\" → true\n- \"Candidates without active job applications\" → false\n- \"Show me candidates with active job applications\" → true\n- \"List all candidates currently in an active application process\" → true\n- \"Find applicants who have not yet been hired or rejected\" → true\n- \"Get candidates with applications that are still in progress\" → true\n- \"Candidates from Google\" → \"\" (not mentioned, leave blank)\n- \"Active candidates\" → \"\" (not active application, leave blank) \n\n---\n\nIs a Prospect\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Indicates whether the candidate is added as a prospect to a requisition.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- prospect\n- added as prospect\n- prospect candidate\n- in prospect pipeline\n- marked as prospects\n- prospects to a requisition\n\n- Do NOT populate `Is a Prospect` when the query only mentions prospect status, status of prospect, or prospect state. Those belong to Prospect Status in the Prospect Tracking subset.\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates added as prospects\" → true\n- \"Candidates who are not prospects\" → false\n- \"prospect status as not interested\" → \"\"\n- \"Show me candidates added as prospects to a requisition\" → true\n- \"Find all prospects linked to open job requisitions\" → true\n- \"List candidates who are marked as prospects in job pipelines\" → true\n- \"Get me all prospect candidates associated with active reqs\" → true\n- \"Who are the prospects added to open positions but haven't applied yet?\" → true\n\n---\n\nIs Referred\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Indicates whether the candidate was referred.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- referred candidate\n- referral\n- employee referral\n- referred by\n- was referred\n\nExamples:\n- \"Referred candidates\" → true\n- \"Candidates who were not referred\" → false\n- \"Find all referred candidates linked to open job requisitions\" → true\n- \"Employee referral candidates\" → true\n\n---\n\nHas Endorsement\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Indicates whether the candidate has endorsements or recommendations.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- endorsed\n- has endorsements\n- has recommendation\n- endorsed internally\n- positive endorsement\n- good recommendations\n- have recommendations\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates with endorsements\" → true\n- \"Candidates without endorsements\" → false\n- \"Show me candidates who have endorsements\" → true\n- \"Find candidates with at least one endorsement or recommendation\" → true\n- \"List all applicants who have been endorsed by someone internally\" → true\n- \"Who are the candidates that have received positive endorsements?\" → true\n- \"Candidates with good recommendations\" → true\n\n---\n\nRehire Recommendation\n\nIMPORTANT: This is NOT a boolean field.\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Allowed values (uppercase only):\n  - \"Y\" → Yes / eligible for rehire / positive rehire status\n  - \"N\" → No / not eligible for rehire / do not rehire\n  - \"ORA_NS\" → Not specified\n- Do not infer rehire eligibility.\n- Always return one of these values in UPPERCASE: Y, N, ORA_NS\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- eligible for rehire\n- rehire recommended\n- positive rehire status\n- not eligible for rehire\n- do not rehire\n- rehire eligible\n- boomerang candidates who left on good terms\n- left voluntarily and have a positive rehire status\n\nExamples:\n- \"Eligible for rehire\" → \"Y\"\n- \"Not eligible for rehire\" → \"N\"\n- \"Rehire status not specified\" → \"ORA_NS\"\n- \"Show me candidates who are eligible for rehire\" → \"Y\"\n- \"Find former employees who left voluntarily and have a positive rehire status\" → \"Y\"\n- \"Give me profiles of boomerang candidates who left on good terms\" → \"Y\"\n- \"Find talent from our alumni network who are rehire eligible\" → \"Y\"\n- \"Candidates marked as do not rehire\" → \"N\"\n\n---\n\nWilling to Relocate\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n- CRITICAL: If followed by a location, DO NOT populate any Location fields. Only populate this field.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- willing to relocate\n- open to relocation\n- willing to move\n- relocation acceptable\n- can move to new city\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates willing to relocate\" → true\n- \"Candidates not willing to relocate\" → false\n- \"Willing to relocate to New York\" → true (Location fields remain blank)\n- \"Find me candidates willing to relocate to SFO\" → true (Location fields remain blank)\n- \"Show me candidates willing to relocate\" → true\n- \"Find candidates open to relocation\" → true\n- \"List applicants who selected yes for relocation in their profile\" → true\n- \"Who is open to moving for a job opportunity?\" → true\n- \"Candidates who can move to new city\" → true\n\n---\n\nWilling to Travel\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n- If followed by a destination, DO NOT populate Location fields.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- willing to travel\n- open to travel\n- travel required\n- comfortable with travel\n- can travel\n- travel availability\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates willing to travel\" → true\n- \"Candidates not willing to travel\" → false\n- \"Show me candidates who are willing to travel\" → true\n- \"Find applicants open to business travel\" → true\n- \"List candidates with travel availability marked as yes or open\" → true\n- \"Who has indicated they're comfortable with travel requirements?\" → true\n- \"Get candidates who selected travel as acceptable in their profile\" → true\n- \"Candidates who can travel\" → true\n\n---\n\nMarketing Communication Opt In\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n- Represents consent to receive marketing communications.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- opted in to marketing\n- marketing consent\n- subscribed to marketing emails\n- opted out of marketing\n- marketing communication opt in\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates opted in for marketing communication\" → true\n- \"Candidates who opted out of marketing\" → false\n- \"Candidates with marketing consent\" → true\n- \"Candidates subscribed to marketing emails\" → true\n\n---\n\nIs Active Candidate\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n- Indicates where a candidate is active or not.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- active candidate\n- inactive candidate\n- candidates who are active\n- candidates who are not active\n- candidate is active\n- candidate is inactive\n- candidate is not active\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates who are acitve\" → true\n- \"Candidates who are not active\" → false\n- \"Candidates with active job applications\" → \"\" (this is not related to active candidates, leave blank)\n\n---\n\nShow Suspicious Candidates Only\n\n- Boolean field.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Accepted values: true or false\n- Indicates whether to return only candidates flagged as suspicious.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- suspicious candidates\n- flagged as suspicious\n- marked as suspicious\n- candidates with suspicious status\n- suspicious profiles\n- show only suspicious candidates\n- filter suspicious candidates\n\nExamples:\n“Show suspicious candidates” → true\n“Only suspicious candidates” → true\n“Candidates flagged as suspicious” → true\n“Candidates with suspicious status” → true\n“High-risk candidates” → \"\"\n“Candidates with fraud risk signals” → \"\"\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nExample 1: Active Applications\nQuery: \"Show me candidates with active job applications\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": true,\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\n\nExample 2: Rehire Eligible with Travel\nQuery: \"Former employees eligible for rehire who are willing to travel\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"Y\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": true,\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\n\nExample 3: Relocation to Specific City\nQuery: \"Find me candidates willing to relocate to SFO\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": true,\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\nNote: Location fields (in Location subset) remain blank.\n\nExample 4: Referred with Endorsements\nQuery: \"Referred candidates with good recommendations\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": true,\n  \"Has Endorsement\": true,\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\n\nExample 5: Not a Prospect\nQuery: \"Candidates who are not prospects\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": false,\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\n\nExample 6: Is Active Candidate\nQuery: \"Active candidates who updated their profiles in the last 90 days\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": true,\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\nNote: \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\" would be populated in the Date subset.\n\nExample 7: Show Suspicious Candidates Only\nQuery: \"Find suspicious candidates who has active job applications\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": true,\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": \"\",\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": \"\",\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\":\"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": true\n}\n\nExample 8: Combination Query\nQuery: \"Find US nationals who can travel and have good recommendations and are not from Microsoft\"\n{\n  \"Has Active Job Applications\": \"\",\n  \"Is a Prospect\": \"\",\n  \"Is Referred\": \"\",\n  \"Has Endorsement\": true,\n  \"Rehire Recommendation\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Relocate\": \"\",\n  \"Willing to Travel\": true,\n  \"Marketing Communication Opt In\": \"\",\n  \"Is Active Candidate\": \"\",\n  \"Show Suspicious Candidates Only\": \"\"\n}\nNote: Citizenship and Employer Excluded fields would be populated in their respective subsets.\n\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate fields outside this subset.\n- Never infer or default values.\n- Never populate false unless explicitly stated (e.g., \"not willing\", \"who are not\", \"without\").\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Excluded Fields"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
          },
          "code": "CANDIDATE_EXCLUDED_FIELDS",
          "id": "1769031948504",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769031948505_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (Excluded Fields Subset: Job Title / Labels / Employer)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query and extract explicitly mentioned exclusion criteria related to job titles, labels, and employers. You must structure the extracted data into the Output JSON format defined below and follow all rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt. It preserves the same behavioral constraints, redundancy, prioritization rules, synonym generation behavior, and enforcement logic, but applies ONLY to the excluded fields listed below.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n\nGlobal Include/Exclude Intent Contract (CRITICAL)\n\n- Parse the query into local clauses (split by punctuation and connectors such as and, but, except, other than, excluding, without, not).\n- For each extracted value in this subset, bind intent at clause level as INCLUDE or EXCLUDE.\n- A value with explicit exclusion intent MUST NOT appear in an include field for the same semantic domain.\n- If a value appears in both include and exclude intent, exclusion intent takes precedence for that value.\n- Exclusion intent is clause-scoped; do not leak negation to unrelated clauses unless explicitly global.\n- Keep schema unchanged and populate only fields defined in this subset.\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate an excluded field ONLY if an explicit exclusion criterion is stated in the query.\n- Do NOT assume, infer, or guess exclusions.\n- Do NOT populate the non-excluded counterpart field when only exclusion is mentioned.\n- Do not add commentary or explanation; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n- Exclusion logic always takes precedence over inclusion logic for the same field.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nGeneral Exclusion Trigger Rules\n\n\n\nExclusion Ownership Rule (CRITICAL):\n- Any label/tag, job title, or employer value mentioned with exclusion intent must be captured only in the corresponding excluded field(s) in this subset.\n- Do not treat exclusion-scoped values as included values in any include counterpart fields.\n- If both include and exclude are present in the same query, capture only explicitly excluded values in this subset and leave include handling to include-focused subsets.\n\nExamples:\n- \"Candidates with Java tag but exclude nursing tag\" -> Labels Excluded: [\"Nursing\"]\n- \"Find candidates with excluded nursing tag\" -> Labels Excluded: [\"Nursing\"]\n\nPopulate excluded fields when the query includes phrases such as (but not limited to):\n\n- not\n- without\n- exclude / excluding\n- except\n- other than\n- never\n- has never\n- have not\n- did not\n- who are not\n- who have not worked at\n- do not have\n- not having\n- has never held\n- has never worked for\n\nNever populate excluded fields unless one of the above exclusion intents is clearly expressed.\n\n---\n\nField-Specific Extraction Rules\n\nJob Title Excluded\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly excludes one or more job titles.\n- Generate synonyms for excluded job titles in the same manner as inclusion logic.\n- Do not return more than 10 job title values.\n- Do NOT populate Job Title (in other subsets) when Job Title Excluded is populated due to exclusion-only queries.\n\nSynonym Generation for Excluded Job Titles:\nApply the same synonym expansion rules as regular job titles. When a job title is excluded, include all common synonyms and related titles.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who are not Software Engineers\"\nJob Title Excluded:\n[\"Software Engineer\", \"Software Developer\", \"Application Developer\", \"Front-end Engineer\", \"Full Stack Engineer\", \"Back-end Engineer\"]\n\nQuery: \"Has never held the job title Deep Sea Diver\"\nJob Title Excluded:\n[\"Deep Sea Diver\", \"Diver\", \"Underwater Expert\", \"Offshore Drilling\"]\n\nQuery: \"Scientists who are not biologists or medical research scientists\"\nJob Title Excluded:\n[\"Biologist\", \"Medical Research Scientist\", \"Research Biologist\", \"Life Science Researcher\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates from Google who are not Software Engineers\"\nJob Title Excluded:\n[\"Software Engineer\", \"Software Developer\", \"Application Developer\", \"Front-end Engineer\", \"Full Stack Engineer\", \"Back-end Engineer\"]\nNote: Employer \"Google\" would be populated in the Employer field in the Professional Background subset.\n\nQuery: \"Scientists from SpaceX exclude biologists or medical research scientists\"\nJob Title Excluded:\n[\"Biologist\", \"Medical Research Scientist\"]\nNote: Job Title \"Scientists\" and Employer \"SpaceX\" would be populated in their respective subsets.\n\n---\n\nLabels Excluded\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly excludes labels or tags.\n- Trigger words MUST explicitly reference label semantics: label, labels, tag, tags, categorized as, grouped as, labeled as\n- Do not infer labels from skills, job titles, or employers.\n- Generate synonyms/related labels when excluding.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates without Nursing tags\"\nLabels Excluded:\n[\"Nurse\", \"Nursing\", \"Cardiac Nurse\", \"Critical Care Nurse\", \"Pediatric Nurse\", \"RN\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates without software engineer label\"\nLabels Excluded:\n[\"Software Engineer\"]\n\nQuery: \"Exclude candidates tagged as management\"\nLabels Excluded:\n[\"Management\", \"Manager\", \"Leadership\"]\n\n---\n\nCurrent Employer Excluded\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly excludes candidates currently working at specific employers.\n- Always expand employer names into synonyms, legal names, abbreviations, stock tickers, subsidiaries, and major brands.\n- Do NOT populate Employer or Former Employer when this field is populated due to exclusion-only queries.\n\nTrigger phrases include (combined with exclusion language):\n- currently working at\n- currently employed by\n- working at\n- employee of\n- is working at\n- works at\n\nEmployer Synonym Expansion (same as inclusion):\n\nGoogle: [\"Google\", \"Alphabet\", \"Google LLC\", \"GOOGL\", \"YouTube\", \"Google Cloud\"]\nMicrosoft: [\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Microsoft Corporation\", \"Azure\"]\nMeta: [\"Meta\", \"Facebook\", \"Instagram\", \"WhatsApp\", \"Meta Platforms\", \"FB\"]\nOracle: [\"Oracle\", \"Oracle Inc\", \"Oracle Corp\", \"Oracle Corporation\", \"ORCL\"]\nAmazon: [\"Amazon\", \"Amazon.com\", \"AWS\", \"Amazon Web Services\", \"AMZN\"]\nNASA: [\"NASA\", \"National Aeronautics and Space Administration\"]\nFederal Government: [\"Federal Government\", \"US Government\", \"Government\", \"Federal Agency\"]\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who are not currently working at Google\"\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"Google\", \"Alphabet\", \"Google LLC\", \"GOOGL\", \"YouTube\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates who are not working at Microsoft\"\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Azure\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who are not from Microsoft\"\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Azure\"]\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Azure\"]\nNote: \"not from\" implies never worked there, so populate both.\n\n---\n\nFormer Employer Excluded\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly excludes candidates who have ever worked at specific employers.\n- Always expand employer names into synonyms, legal names, abbreviations, stock tickers, subsidiaries, and major brands.\n- If the exclusion implies both past and present employment (e.g., \"has never worked for\"), populate BOTH Former Employer Excluded AND Current Employer Excluded.\n\nTrigger phrases include (combined with exclusion language):\n- worked at\n- formerly worked at\n- previously worked at\n- employed by\n- has worked at\n- have worked at\n- ever worked at\n\nCritical Rule - \"Never Worked\" Implies Both:\nWhen the query uses phrases like \"has never worked for\", \"have not worked at\", or \"never worked at\", populate BOTH Former Employer Excluded AND Current Employer Excluded with the same values.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who have not formerly worked at Macy's\"\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"Macys\", \"Macys Inc\", \"Macy's Department Store\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who have never worked for Oracle\"\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"Oracle\", \"Oracle Inc\", \"Oracle Corp\", \"Oracle Corporation\", \"ORCL\"]\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"Oracle\", \"Oracle Inc\", \"Oracle Corp\", \"Oracle Corporation\", \"ORCL\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who have not worked at NASA or the Federal Government\"\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"NASA\", \"Federal Government\", \"US Government\"]\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"NASA\", \"Federal Government\", \"US Government\"]\n\nQuery: \"Scientists who have not worked in NASA or Federal Government\"\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"NASA\", \"Federal Government\", \"US Government\", \"Government Agency\"]\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"NASA\", \"Federal Government\", \"US Government\", \"Government Agency\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who have not worked in Macys\"\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"Macys\", \"Macys Inc\"]\nCurrent Employer Excluded:\n[\"Macys\", \"Macys Inc\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who have formerly worked at Macys but are now working at JC Penny\"\nNote: This query has NO exclusion. Former Employer: [\"Macys\", \"Macys Inc\"], Current Employer: [\"JC Penny\", \"JCP\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who have not formerly worked at Macys but are now working at JC Penny\"\nFormer Employer Excluded:\n[\"Macys\", \"Macys Inc\"]\nNote: Current Employer \"JC Penny\" would be populated in Professional Background subset.\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nExample 1: Job Title Exclusion Only\nQuery: \"Scientists who are not biologists or medical research scientists\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [\"Biologist\", \"Medical Research Scientist\", \"Research Biologist\", \"Life Science Researcher\"],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": []\n}\n\nExample 2: Never Worked At (Both Employer Fields)\nQuery: \"Candidates who have not worked at NASA or the Federal Government\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [\"NASA\", \"Federal Government\", \"US Government\", \"Government Agency\"],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": [\"NASA\", \"Federal Government\", \"US Government\", \"Government Agency\"]\n}\n\nExample 3: Labels Exclusion\nQuery: \"Find candidates without Nursing tags\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [\"Nurse\", \"Nursing\", \"Cardiac Nurse\", \"Critical Care Nurse\", \"Pediatric Nurse\"],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": []\n}\n\nExample 4: Complex Exclusion with Multiple Fields\nQuery: \"Scientists from SpaceX sourced from Social media who have PHD exclude biologists. Who have not worked in NASA\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [\"Biologist\", \"Medical Research Scientist\", \"Life Scientist\"],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [\"NASA\", \"National Aeronautics and Space Administration\"],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": [\"NASA\", \"National Aeronautics and Space Administration\"]\n}\nNote: Job Title \"Scientists\", Employer \"SpaceX\", Medium \"Social Media\", and Degree \"PhD\" would be populated in their respective subsets.\n\nExample 5: Current Employer Exclusion Only\nQuery: \"Find candidates not currently working at Google\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [\"Google\", \"Alphabet\", \"Google LLC\", \"GOOGL\"],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": []\n}\n\nExample 6: Combined Job Title and Employer Exclusion\nQuery: \"Candidates from Google who are not Software Engineers and have never worked at Microsoft\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [\"Software Engineer\", \"Software Developer\", \"Application Developer\", \"Front-end Engineer\", \"Full Stack Engineer\", \"Back-end Engineer\"],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Azure\"],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": [\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Azure\"]\n}\nNote: Employer \"Google\" would be populated in Professional Background subset.\n\nExample 7: Not From Employer (Implies Never Worked)\nQuery: \"Find US nationals in San Francisco who are not from Microsoft\"\n{\n  \"Job Title Excluded\": [],\n  \"Labels Excluded\": [],\n  \"Current Employer Excluded\": [\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Microsoft Corporation\", \"Azure\"],\n  \"Former Employer Excluded\": [\"Microsoft\", \"MSFT\", \"Microsoft Corp\", \"Microsoft Corporation\", \"Azure\"]\n}\nNote: Location and Citizenship would be populated in their respective subsets.\n\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate non-excluded fields in this subset.\n- Never infer exclusions.\n- Always expand synonyms for excluded values (same expansion rules as inclusion).\n- When \"never worked\" is implied, populate BOTH Current and Former Employer Excluded.\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Internal Structure"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
          },
          "code": "CANDIDATE_INTERNAL_STRUCTURE",
          "id": "1769031972461",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769031972463_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (Requisitions / Pools / Events / Source / Medium Subset)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query, extract explicitly mentioned pipeline, campaign, and sourcing-related identifiers, and structure them into the Output JSON format defined below. Follow all rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt. It preserves the same behavioral constraints, redundancy, precedence rules, and enforcement logic, but applies ONLY to the fields listed in the Output JSON Format section.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate a field ONLY if it is explicitly mentioned in the query.\n- Do NOT infer, assume, or guess values.\n- Do NOT populate related fields when one of these fields is active unless explicitly instructed.\n- Do not add commentary or explanation; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n- Certain fields in this subset are mutually exclusive; enforce this strictly.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nMutual Exclusivity Rules (CRITICAL)\n\nRequisitions, Pools, and Events are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE:\n\n- If one of these fields is populated, the other two MUST remain empty.\n- Always give precedence to the field explicitly referenced by keyword:\n  - Keywords: requisition, req, job, applied to → Requisitions\n  - Keywords: pool, talent pool → Pools\n  - Keywords: event, attended → Events\n- Never populate more than one of these three fields.\n\nCRITICAL RULE - No Other Fields When Req/Pool/Event is Populated:\nDo NOT populate any other fields (Job Title, Employer, Skills, etc.) when a Requisition, Pool, or Event identifier is extracted, EVEN IF the identifier overlaps with known job titles, employer names, or skills.\n\nExamples:\n- \"Find me candidates from Java Developer Pool\" → Pools: [\"Java Developer\"], Skills: [], Job Title: []\n- \"Find me candidates from Samsung Hiring Event\" → Events: [\"Samsung Hiring\"], Employer: []\n- \"Find me candidates who applied to Cardiac Nurse\" → Requisitions: [\"Cardiac Nurse\"], Skills: [], Job Title: []\n\n---\n\nRequisitions\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly references a job, requisition, req, or application identifier.\n- Accept one or more identifiers.\n- STRICTLY: Do NOT suffix values with the words \"job\" or \"requisition\".\n- Extract only the meaningful identifier text.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- applied to requisitions\n- applied to job\n- applied to req\n- candidates who applied to\n- from requisition\n\nSuffix Stripping Rule:\nRemove trailing words: \"job\", \"requisition\", \"req\", \"position\"\n- \"Python Developer job\" → \"Python Developer\"\n- \"Cardiac Nurse requisition\" → \"Cardiac Nurse\"\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates who applied to requisitions xyz or abc\"\nRequisitions: [\"xyz\", \"abc\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who applied to Python Developer job\"\nRequisitions: [\"Python Developer\"]\nNote: Job Title and Skills remain empty.\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who applied to Cardiac Nurse\"\nRequisitions: [\"Cardiac Nurse\"]\nNote: Job Title and Skills remain empty.\n\nQuery: \"Candidates from Software Engineer requisition\"\nRequisitions: [\"Software Engineer\"]\nNote: Job Title remains empty.\n\n---\n\nPools\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly references a pool or talent pool.\n- Accept one or more identifiers.\n- STRICTLY: Do NOT suffix values with the word \"pool\".\n- Extract only the meaningful identifier text.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- part of pool\n- from pool\n- in pool\n- talent pool\n- candidate pool\n\nSuffix Stripping Rule:\nRemove trailing word: \"pool\"\n- \"Global Pool\" → \"Global\"\n- \"Java Developer Pool\" → \"Java Developer\"\n- \"Python Developer Pool\" → \"Python Developer\"\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who are part of Global Pool\"\nPools: [\"Global\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates from Java Developer Pool\"\nPools: [\"Java Developer\"]\nNote: Skills and Job Title remain empty.\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who are part of Pool Python Developer\"\nPools: [\"Python Developer\"]\nNote: Skills and Job Title remain empty.\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates from Java Developer Pool\"\nPools: [\"Java Developer\"]\nSkills: []\nJob Title: []\n\n---\n\nEvents\n\n- Populate only if the query explicitly references an event.\n- Accept one or more identifiers.\n- STRICTLY: Do NOT suffix values with the word \"event\".\n- Extract only the meaningful identifier text.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- attended event\n- from event\n- part of event\n- registered for event\n- event members\n\nSuffix Stripping Rule:\nRemove trailing word: \"event\"\n- \"JPMC Hiring Event\" → \"JPMC Hiring\"\n- \"Samsung Hiring Event\" → \"Samsung Hiring\"\n- \"nature event\" → \"nature\"\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who attended JPMC Hiring Event\"\nEvents: [\"JPMC Hiring\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates from nature event\"\nEvents: [\"nature\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates from Samsung Hiring Event\"\nEvents: [\"Samsung Hiring\"]\nEmployer: []\nNote: Employer remains empty even though \"Samsung\" is a company name.\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who attended xyz or abc events\"\nEvents: [\"xyz\", \"abc\"]\n\n---\n\nSource\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Represents the origin from which the candidate entered the system.\n- Extract literal source names (e.g., LinkedIn, Indeed, Referral).\n- This field is INDEPENDENT of Requisitions, Pools, and Events.\n- Source can be populated alongside any of the three mutually exclusive fields above.\n\nTrigger phrases include:\n- sourced from\n- source is\n- source as\n- came from\n- applied from\n- sourced via\n\nCommon Source Values (examples only, not exhaustive):\n- LinkedIn\n- Indeed\n- Glassdoor\n- Referral\n- Career Site\n- Job Board\n- Agency\n- Campus Recruiting\n- Email\n- Phone\n\n- Extract any explicitly provided source value, even if it is not listed above.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"get me candidates with source as email or phone\"\nSource: [\"email\", \"phone\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates sourced from LinkedIn\"\nSource: [\"LinkedIn\"]\n\nQuery: \"Temporary workers who applied from Indeed\"\nSource: [\"Indeed\"]\n\nQuery: \"Scientists from SpaceX sourced from Social media and LinkedIn\"\nSource: [\"LinkedIn\"]\nMedium: [\"social\"]\nNote: Employer \"SpaceX\" and Job Title \"Scientists\" would be populated in their respective subsets.\n\n---\n\nMedium\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Represents the channel or medium type through which the candidate was sourced.\n- Use normalized channel values when implied.\n- This field is INDEPENDENT of Requisitions, Pools, and Events.\n\nCommon Medium Mappings:\n\n| User Input | Medium Value |\n|------------|--------------|\n| social media | \"social\" |\n| social | \"social\" |\n| LinkedIn (when medium context) | \"social\" |\n| agency | \"agency\" |\n| staffing agency | \"agency\" |\n| job fair | \"job-fair\" |\n| career fair | \"job-fair\" |\n| career site | \"career-site\" |\n| company website | \"career-site\" |\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Candidates sourced from social media\"\nMedium: [\"social\"]\n\nQuery: \"Show candidates from job fairs\"\nMedium: [\"job-fair\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates sourced from agencies or social media based out of California\"\nMedium: [\"agency\", \"social\"]\nNote: Location would be populated in Location subset.\n\nQuery: \"Show candidates from job fairs who have skills in nursing\"\nMedium: [\"job-fair\"]\nNote: Skills would be populated in Professional Background subset.\n\n---\n\nSource + Medium Interaction Rules\n\n- Source and Medium can BOTH be populated if both are explicitly mentioned.\n- Do NOT infer Medium from Source unless explicitly stated.\n- Do NOT infer Source from Medium unless explicitly stated.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates sourced from LinkedIn via social media\"\nSource: [\"LinkedIn\"]\nMedium: [\"social\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates from agencies\"\nMedium: [\"agency\"]\nSource: [] (empty - not specified)\n\nQuery: \"Candidates sourced from Indeed through job boards\"\nSource: [\"Indeed\"]\nMedium: [\"job-board\"]\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nExample 1: Requisition Only\nQuery: \"Find candidates who applied to Python Developer job\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [\"Python Developer\"],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\n\nExample 2: Pool Only\nQuery: \"Candidates who are part of Global Pool\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [\"Global\"],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\n\nExample 3: Event Only\nQuery: \"Candidates who attended JPMC Hiring Event\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [\"JPMC Hiring\"],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\n\nExample 4: Source and Medium Together\nQuery: \"Find candidates sourced from LinkedIn via social media\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [\"LinkedIn\"],\n  \"Medium\": [\"social\"]\n}\n\nExample 5: Medium with Skills (in other subset)\nQuery: \"Show candidates from job fairs who have skills in nursing\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": [\"job-fair\"]\n}\nNote: Skills: [\"Nursing\", ...] would be in Professional Background subset.\n\nExample 6: Event with Employer Name (DO NOT populate Employer)\nQuery: \"Find me candidates from Samsung Hiring Event\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [\"Samsung Hiring\"],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\nNote: Employer remains empty in Professional Background subset.\n\nExample 7: Pool with Skill Name (DO NOT populate Skills)\nQuery: \"Find me candidates from Java Developer Pool\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [\"Java Developer\"],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\nNote: Skills and Job Title remain empty in Professional Background subset.\n\nExample 8: Multiple Requisitions\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who applied to requisitions xyz or abc\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [\"xyz\", \"abc\"],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": []\n}\n\nExample 9: Multiple Mediums with Location\nQuery: \"Find candidates sourced from agencies or social media based out of California\"\n{\n  \"Requisitions\": [],\n  \"Pools\": [],\n  \"Events\": [],\n  \"Source\": [],\n  \"Medium\": [\"agency\", \"social\"]\n}\nNote: Location would be populated in Location subset with California.\n\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate fields outside this subset.\n- Never infer identifiers.\n- Strictly enforce mutual exclusivity for Requisitions / Pools / Events.\n- Always strip suffix words (event, pool, job, requisition) from identifiers.\n- Do NOT populate other fields (Job Title, Skills, Employer) when Req/Pool/Event overlaps with those values.\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Evaluation Data"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
