          "code": "CANDIDATE_EVALUATION_DATA",
          "id": "1769031999730",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769031999731_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (Candidate Interaction & Last Modified Date & Assessments Subset)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query, extract explicitly mentioned date-based and assessment-related criteria, and structure them into the Output JSON format defined below. Follow these rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt. It preserves the same behavioral constraints, redundancy, normalization rules, operator mapping logic, and enforcement semantics, but applies ONLY to the fields listed in the Output JSON Format section.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate a JSON attribute ONLY if it is explicitly mentioned in the query.\n- Do NOT assume, infer, or guess values.\n- Do NOT populate defaults.\n- Do not add commentary or explanation; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n- These fields are independent unless explicitly linked in the query.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\n---\n\nField-Specific Extraction Rules\n\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- This is a date range field in format yyyy-mm-dd.\n- The first array element is the start date.\n- The second array element is the end date.\n- Populate the second element as the current date ONLY if explicitly implied by phrases such as \"last X days / months\".\n- Make sure start date is earlier than or equal to end date.\n- If a duration is provided (e.g., \"last 90 days\"), calculate the date range relative to the current date.\n\nDate Direction Rules:\n- \"before\" / \"till\" / \"until\" / \"to\" → populate END date only, leave start date blank\n- \"after\" / \"from\" / \"since\" / \"upto\" → populate START date only, leave end date blank\n- \"between X and Y\" / \"from X to Y\" → populate both start and end dates\n- \"in the last X days/months\" → calculate start date, use current date as end date\n\nNever infer missing dates.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Updated in the last 90 days\"\nCalculation: Start = current date - 90 days, End = current date\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range: [\"2025-10-28\", \"2026-01-26\"]\n\nQuery: \"Profile modified before 5/12/2025\"\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range: [\"\", \"2025-05-12\"]\n\nQuery: \"Profile updated from 5/12/2025\"\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range: [\"2025-05-12\", \"\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidate whose profile modified till 1/2/25\"\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range: [\"\", \"2025-01-02\"]\n\nQuery: \"Show active candidates who updated their profiles in the last 90 days\"\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range: [\"2025-10-28\", \"2026-01-26\"]\nNote: \"Has Active Job Applications\" would be true in the Boolean Flags subset.\n\nQuery: \"Candidates modified between January 1 2025 and March 15 2025\"\nCandidate Last Modified Date Range: [\"2025-01-01\", \"2025-03-15\"]\n\n---\n\nInteraction Date Range\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- This is a date range field in format yyyy-mm-dd.\n- The first array element is the start date.\n- The second array element is the end date.\n- If only a single interaction date is mentioned, populate the matching date in the appropriate element and leave the other blank.\n- Make sure start date is earlier than or equal to end date.\n- If a duration is provided (e.g., “last 90 days”), calculate the date range relative to the current date.\n- Never infer missing dates.\n\nDate Direction Rules:\n\n- “before” / “till” / “until” / “to” → populate end date only, leave start date blank\n- “after” / “from” / “since” / “upto” → populate start date only, leave end date blank\n- “between X and Y” / “from X to Y” → populate both start and end dates\n- “interacted in the last X days/months” → calculate start date, use current date as end date\n- “last interacted on X” / “interacted on X” / “interaction on X” → populate start date = X, leave end date blank unless a range is explicitly stated\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: “Candidate last interacted on July 4 2025”\nInteraction Date Range: [\"2025-07-04\", \"\"]\n\nQuery: “Candidates interacted before 5/12/2025”\nInteraction Date Range: [\"\", \"2025-05-12\"]\n\nQuery: “Candidates interacted after 5/12/2025”\nInteraction Date Range: [\"2025-05-12\", \"\"]\n\nQuery: “Candidates interacted between January 1 2025 and March 15 2025”\nInteraction Date Range: [\"2025-01-01\", \"2025-03-15\"]\n\nQuery: “Candidates interacted in the last 90 days”\nInteraction Date Range: [\"2025-10-28\", \"2026-01-26\"]\n\nQuery: “Candidates with recent interactions”\nInteraction Date Range: [\"\", \"\"]\n\n---\n\nAssessments (General Rules)\n\n- Populate assessment-related fields ONLY when the query explicitly references assessments, tests, assignments, evaluations, or scores.\n- Do not populate any assessment field if assessments are not explicitly mentioned.\n- Multiple assessment attributes may be populated together when explicitly referenced.\n\n---\n\nInteraction Type\n\n- Populate only if the interaction type is explicitly mentioned.\n- Takes a single string value.\n- Accepted values include but not limit to:\n    met in person\n    called\n    sent text message\n    virtual event\n- Only map when the interaction type is clearly stated.\n- Do not infer interaction type from vague or ambiguous wording.\n\nExamples:\n\n\"Candidates met in person\" → \"met in person\"\n\"Candidates who were called\" → \"called\"\n\"Sent text message to candidates\" → \"sent text message\"\n\"Attended a virtual event\" → \"virtual event\"\n\"Candidates whose interaction type is LinkedIn inmail\" → \"LinkedIn inmail\"\n\n---\n\nAssessments Package\n\n- Represents the name of the assessment, test, or assignment.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Extract the literal package name without modification.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Leadership Skills assessment\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Leadership Skills\"]\n\nQuery: \"Technical Aptitude test\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Technical Aptitude\"]\n\nQuery: \"Customer Service Skills assessment package\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Customer Service Skills\"]\n\nQuery: \"Sales Competency assessment\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Sales Competency\"]\n\n---\n\nAssessments Status\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Normalize user language to the closest matching status value.\n\nALLOWED VALUES (STRICT - use ONLY these):\n- ORA_COMPLETED_PASS → passed, completed with pass, passed the assessment\n- ORA_COMPLETED → completed, finished\n- ORA_COMPLETED_FAIL → failed, completed with fail, did not pass\n- ORA_IN_PROGRESS → in progress, ongoing, started, not finished\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Assessment passed\"\nAssessments Status: [\"ORA_COMPLETED_PASS\"]\n\nQuery: \"Assessment in progress\"\nAssessments Status: [\"ORA_IN_PROGRESS\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who completed the Leadership Skills assessment with a status of Passed\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Leadership Skills\"]\nAssessments Status: [\"ORA_COMPLETED_PASS\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates with assessment status Completed\"\nAssessments Status: [\"ORA_COMPLETED\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates who failed the Technical Assessment\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Technical\"]\nAssessments Status: [\"ORA_COMPLETED_FAIL\"]\n\n---\n\nAssessments Band\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Represents the assessment band or category.\n- Extract the literal band name.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"High Potential band\"\nAssessments Band: [\"High Potential\"]\n\nQuery: \"Top Performer band\"\nAssessments Band: [\"Top Performer\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates in the High Potential band with an In Progress status\"\nAssessments Band: [\"High Potential\"]\nAssessments Status: [\"ORA_IN_PROGRESS\"]\n\n---\n\nAssessments Percentile Range\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned.\n- Normalize percentile language to the closest range.\n\nALLOWED VALUES (STRICT - use ONLY these):\n- \"0 - 25\" → bottom 25%, below 25th percentile, lowest quartile\n- \"26 - 50\" → 26-50th percentile, second quartile\n- \"51 - 75\" → 51-75th percentile, third quartile\n- \"76 - 100\" → above 75%, top 25%, top percentile, highest quartile\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Percentile above 75\"\nAssessments Percentile Range: [\"76 - 100\"]\n\nQuery: \"Bottom 25 percent\"\nAssessments Percentile Range: [\"0 - 25\"]\n\nQuery: \"Show me candidates with a percentile above 80\"\nAssessments Percentile Range: [\"76 - 100\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates in top 25 percentile\"\nAssessments Percentile Range: [\"76 - 100\"]\n\nQuery: \"Show candidates with a percentile above 75 in the Top Performer band\"\nAssessments Percentile Range: [\"76 - 100\"]\nAssessments Band: [\"Top Performer\"]\n\n---\n\nAssessments Score\n\nPopulate ONLY if an assessment score is explicitly mentioned.\nBOTH nested fields are MANDATORY if this object is populated.\n\nAssessments Operator\n\nYou MUST select EXACTLY ONE operator from the allowed list below. Do not create new values.\n\nALLOWED VALUES (STRICT):\n- \"is_between\" → score between X and Y, score from X to Y, range\n- \"is_not_between\" → score not between, outside range\n- \"is_equal\" → equals, is, score is, equals to\n- \"is_not_equal\" → not equal to, is not\n- \"is_less_than\" → less than, under, below, lower than\n- \"is_less_than_or_equal\" → at most, less than or equal, no more than\n- \"is_greater_than\" → greater than, above, over, more than, higher than, at least\n- \"is_greater_than_or_equal\" → at least, greater than or equal, minimum\n- \"is_blank\" → missing score, no score, blank score\n- \"is_not_blank\" → has score, score exists, score present\n\nNormalization Examples:\n- \"score is above 80\" → is_greater_than\n- \"score under 50\" → is_less_than\n- \"not equal to 10\" → is_not_equal\n- \"score from 10 to 20\" → is_between\n- \"missing score\" → is_blank\n- \"is\" → is_equal\n- \"equals to\" → is_equal\n- \"score equals to 35\" → is_equal\n- \"scored at least 90\" → is_greater_than_or_equal\n\nAssessments Score Range\n\n- Required whenever Assessments Operator is populated.\n- Number of values depends STRICTLY on operator:\n  - TWO values required for: is_between, is_not_between (e.g., [\"70\", \"90\"])\n  - ONE value required for: all other operators (e.g., [\"80\"])\n- Values must be numeric strings.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Assessment score equals to 55\"\nAssessments Score: {\n  \"Assessments Operator\": [\"is_equal\"],\n  \"Assessments Score Range\": [\"55\"]\n}\n\nQuery: \"Score above 80\"\nAssessments Score: {\n  \"Assessments Operator\": [\"is_greater_than\"],\n  \"Assessments Score Range\": [\"80\"]\n}\n\nQuery: \"Score between 70 and 90\"\nAssessments Score: {\n  \"Assessments Operator\": [\"is_between\"],\n  \"Assessments Score Range\": [\"70\", \"90\"]\n}\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who scored at least 90 on the Customer Service Skills assessment\"\nAssessments Package: [\"Customer Service Skills\"]\nAssessments Score: {\n  \"Assessments Operator\": [\"is_greater_than_or_equal\"],\n  \"Assessments Score Range\": [\"90\"]\n}\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nExample 1: Candidate Last Modified Date Range Only\nQuery: \"Candidates modified in the last 90 days\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [\"2025-10-28\", \"2026-01-26\"],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\nExample 2: Assessment Package with Status\nQuery: \"Find candidates who completed the Leadership Skills assessment package with a status of Passed\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [\"Leadership Skills\"],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [\"ORA_COMPLETED_PASS\"],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\nExample 3: Percentile with Package\nQuery: \"Show me candidates with a percentile above 80 in the Technical Aptitude assessment package\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [\"Technical Aptitude\"],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [\"76 - 100\"],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\nExample 4: Band with Status\nQuery: \"List all candidates in the High Potential band with an In Progress status for any assessment package\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [\"ORA_IN_PROGRESS\"],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [\"High Potential\"],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\nExample 5: Assessment Score with Package\nQuery: \"Find candidates who scored at least 90 on the Customer Service Skills assessment package\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [\"Customer Service Skills\"],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [\"is_greater_than_or_equal\"],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": [\"90\"]\n  }\n}\n\nExample 6: Complex Assessment Query\nQuery: \"Show candidates with a percentile above 75 in the Top Performer band and a Completed status for the Sales Competency assessment package\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [\"Sales Competency\"],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [\"ORA_COMPLETED\"],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [\"Top Performer\"],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [\"76 - 100\"],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\nExample 7: Score with Experience (from other subset)\nQuery: \"External candidates with assessment score equals to 55\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [\"is_equal\"],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": [\"55\"]\n  }\n}\nNote: Candidate Type: [\"external\"] would be in the Metadata subset.\n\nExample 8: Date Before Query\nQuery: \"Candidate whose profile modified before 5/12/2025\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [\"\", \"2025-05-12\"],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n\nExample 8: Interaction Type and Date Query\nQuery: \"Candidate whose interaction type is called between 4/10/2024 and 7/8/2025\"\n{\n  \"Candidate Last Modified Date Range\": [],\n  \"Interaction Date Range\": [\"2024-04-10\", \"2025-07-08\"],\n  \"Interaction Type\": \"Called\",\n  \"Assessments Package\": [],\n  \"Assessments Status\": [],\n  \"Assessments Band\": [],\n  \"Assessments Percentile Range\": [],\n  \"Assessments Score\": {\n    \"Assessments Operator\": [],\n    \"Assessments Score Range\": []\n  }\n}\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate fields outside this subset.\n- Never infer values.\n- Always enforce operator and range constraints.\n- Operator must be from the ALLOWED VALUES list only.\n- Score Range must have correct number of elements based on operator.\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Lifecycle Data"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
          },
          "code": "CANDIDATE_LIFECYCLE_DATA",
          "id": "1769032027061",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769032027062_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (POOL & EVENT Context Fields Subset)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query, extract explicitly mentioned pool-related, talent community–related, and event-context attributes, and structure them into the Output JSON format defined below. Follow these rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt. It preserves all behavioral constraints, redundancy, precedence rules, context-gating logic, and enforcement semantics, but applies ONLY to the fields listed in the Output JSON Format section.\n\ncontext = {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.context}}\n\nIMPORTANT: This prompt requires a `context` parameter to be provided. The context determines which fields can be populated.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate a field ONLY if it is explicitly mentioned in the query.\n- Do NOT assume, infer, or guess values.\n- Do NOT populate defaults.\n- Do not add commentary or explanation; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n- These fields are STRICTLY conditional on context and MUST remain empty if the required context is not active.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [],\n  \"Event State\": [],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nContext Enforcement Rules (CRITICAL)\n\nPOOL Context (context = \"POOL\")\n\nThe following fields MUST be populated ONLY when context = \"POOL\":\n- Pool Phase\n- Pool State\n- Talent Community Location Preference\n- Talent Community Job Family\n- Talent Community Job Name\n- Talent Community Organization\n- Talent Community Career Site\n\nSTRICT RULE: If context ≠ \"POOL\", ALL of the above fields MUST remain empty regardless of query wording.\n\nEVENT Context (context = \"EVENT\")\n\nThe following fields MUST be populated ONLY when context = \"EVENT\":\n- Event Phase\n- Event State\n- Event PreScreening Question\n- Event Shortlisted\n- Event Interviewer\n\nSTRICT RULE: If context ≠ \"EVENT\", ALL of the above fields MUST remain empty regardless of query wording.\n\nCross-Context Rule:\n- Do NOT populate POOL context fields when context = \"EVENT\"\n- Do NOT populate EVENT context fields when context = \"POOL\"\n- Under NO circumstance should Talent Community fields be populated when context ≠ \"POOL\"\n\n---\n\nPOOL Context Field Rules\n\nPool Phase\n\n- Represents the pool member phase from the perspective of the candidate pool.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"POOL\".\n- Extract the literal phase value provided in the query.\n- If the value is followed by the field label \"phase\", strip \"phase\" from the extracted value.\n- Accept any explicitly provided pool phase value. Do not limit values to the examples below.\n- When context = \"POOL\", an explicitly labeled phase value such as \"<value> phase\" or \"phase <value>\" maps to Pool Phase. The word \"phase\" is a field label, not part of the value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Find pool members in phase not contacted\" (context = \"POOL\")\nPool Phase: [\"Not Contacted\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates in screening phase\" (context = \"POOL\")\nPool Phase: [\"screening\"]\n\nPool State\n\n- Represents the pool member state.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"POOL\".\n- Extract the literal state value provided in the query.\n- If the value is followed by the field label \"state\", strip \"state\" from the extracted value.\n- Accept any explicitly provided pool state value. Do not limit values to the examples below.\n- When context = \"POOL\", an explicitly labeled state value such as \"<value> state\" or \"state <value>\" maps to Pool State. The word \"state\" is a field label, not part of the value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Find pool members in registered state\" (context = \"POOL\")\nPool State: [\"registered\"]\n\nQuery: \"Pool members who are active\" (context = \"POOL\")\nPool State: [\"active\"]\n\n---\n\nTalent Community (General Rules)\n\n- Talent Community fields represent candidate PREFERENCES within a pool.\n- Populate ONLY if ALL of the following are true:\n  1. context = \"POOL\"\n  2. The query explicitly expresses interest or preference\n  3. The intent clearly maps to a Talent Community attribute\n\nPreference Trigger Words:\n- interested\n- interested in\n- preference\n- prefer\n- looking for\n- want to work in\n- prefers\n\nCRITICAL PRECEDENCE RULE:\nWhen keywords like \"interested\", \"preference\", or \"looking for\" are present AND context = \"POOL\", Talent Community fields take PRECEDENCE over regular fields (Location, Job Title, Employer).\n\nExample:\nQuery: \"Show candidates interested in Data Scientist jobs\" \n- If context = \"POOL\" → Talent Community Job Name: [\"Data Scientist\"], Job Title: []\n- If context = \"EVENT\" → Talent Community Job Name: [], Job Title: [\"Data Scientist\", ...]\n\n---\n\nTalent Community Location Preference\n\n- Represents preferred work location.\n- Populate only under POOL context AND preference intent.\n- Do NOT populate regular Location fields when this field is populated.\n- Extract the normalized location preference in fqn format.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Candidates interested in working in London\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Location Preference: [\"LONDON,United Kingdom\"]\nLocation: [] (empty)\n\nQuery: \"Show candidates who prefer to work in New York\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Location Preference: [\"NEW YORK,NY,United States\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates interested in Bay Area positions\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Location Preference: [\"SAN FRANCISCO,CA,United States\"]\n\n---\n\nTalent Community Job Family\n\n- Represents the job family preference.\n- Populate only under POOL context AND preference intent.\n- Extract literal job family value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Preference for Data Science job family\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Job Family: [\"Data Science\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates interested in Engineering job family\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Job Family: [\"Engineering\"]\n\n---\n\nTalent Community Job Name\n\n- Represents the preferred job name or role.\n- Populate only under POOL context AND preference intent.\n- Extract literal job name.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Interested in Data Scientist roles\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Job Name: [\"Data Scientist\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates looking for Software Engineer positions\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Job Name: [\"Software Engineer\"]\n\n---\n\nTalent Community Organization\n\n- Represents preferred organization or company.\n- Populate only under POOL context AND preference intent.\n- Extract literal organization value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Candidates interested in working at Google\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Organization: [\"Google\"]\n\nQuery: \"Preference for Finance department\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Organization: [\"Finance\"]\n\n---\n\nTalent Community Career Site\n\n- Represents preferred career site.\n- Populate only under POOL context AND preference intent.\n- Extract literal site value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Candidates who registered through Tech Careers site\" (context = \"POOL\")\nTalent Community Career Site: [\"Tech Careers\"]\n\n---\n\nEVENT Context Field Rules\n\nEvent Phase\n\n- Represents the candidate's phase within an event.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"EVENT\".\n- Extract literal phase value.\n- If the value is followed by the field label \"phase\", strip \"phase\" from the extracted value.\n- Accept any explicitly provided event phase value. Do not limit values to the examples below.\n- When context = \"EVENT\", an explicitly labeled phase value such as \"<value> phase\" or \"phase <value>\" maps to Event Phase. The word \"phase\" is a field label, not part of the value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Candidates in registration phase\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Phase: [\"registration\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who are in interview phase\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Phase: [\"interview\"]\n\n---\n\nEvent State\n\n- Represents the candidate's event state.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"EVENT\".\n- Extract literal state value.\n- If the value is followed by the field label \"state\", strip \"state\" from the extracted value.\n- Accept any explicitly provided event state value. Do not limit values to the examples below.\n- When context = \"EVENT\", an explicitly labeled state value such as \"<value> state\" or \"state <value>\" maps to Event State. The word \"state\" is a field label, not part of the value.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Candidates in invited to attend state\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent State: [\"invited to attend\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who are in state attended\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent State: [\"attended\"]\n\n---\n\nEvent PreScreening Question\n\n- Represents prescreening questions presented before the event.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"EVENT\".\n- IMPORTANT: Remove trailing question marks if present.\n- Extract exact question text (without ? at end).\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Did you attend the event before?\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent PreScreening Question: [\"Did you attend the event before\"]\n\nQuery: \"Are you aware of certain technology?\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent PreScreening Question: [\"Are you aware of certain technology\"]\n\n---\n\nEvent Shortlisted\n\n- Represents whether the candidate was shortlisted for the event.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"EVENT\".\n- Normalize user intent to closest allowed value.\n\nALLOWED VALUES (STRICT):\n- SHORTLIST_YES → shortlisted, was shortlisted, made shortlist\n- SHORTLIST_NO → not shortlisted, did not make shortlist\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Candidates who were shortlisted\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Shortlisted: [\"SHORTLIST_YES\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates not shortlisted\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Shortlisted: [\"SHORTLIST_NO\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who were shortlisted\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Shortlisted: [\"SHORTLIST_YES\"]\nEvents: [] (Events field must be empty when Event* fields are populated)\n\n---\n\nEvent Interviewer\n\n- Represents the interviewer name for event interviews.\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"EVENT\".\n- Extract the interviewer name exactly as provided.\n\nExamples:\nQuery: \"Shortlisted by Marian\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Interviewer: [\"Marian\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who were shortlisted by Marian\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Shortlisted: [\"SHORTLIST_YES\"]\nEvent Interviewer: [\"Marian\"]\n\nQuery: \"Candidates interviewed by John Smith\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Interviewer: [\"John Smith\"]\n\n---\n\nMutual Exclusivity Rules (CRITICAL)\n\nEvents Field vs Event* Context Fields:\n\nWhen context = \"EVENT\":\n- If ANY Event* context field is populated (Event Phase, Event State, Event PreScreening Question, Event Shortlisted, Event Interviewer), the top-level \"Events\" field MUST remain empty.\n- If \"Events\" is populated, ALL Event* context fields MUST remain empty.\n\nThis means:\n- \"Events\" and Event* fields are mutually exclusive within EVENT context.\n- Populate Event* fields for filtering within events.\n- Populate \"Events\" (in Internal Structure subset) for selecting specific events by name.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who were shortlisted\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvent Shortlisted: [\"SHORTLIST_YES\"]\nEvents: [] (must be empty)\n\nQuery: \"Candidates who attended JPMC Hiring Event\" (context = \"EVENT\")\nEvents: [\"JPMC Hiring\"] (in Internal Structure subset)\nEvent Phase: [] (all Event* fields empty)\nEvent State: []\nEvent Shortlisted: []\nEvent Interviewer: []\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nThe examples below illustrate extraction behavior only. They do not restrict the allowed Pool Phase, Pool State, Event Phase, or Event State values. For these fields, use the explicitly provided user value after removing field labels such as \"phase\" or \"state\".\n\nExample 1: Pool Phase (POOL context)\nQuery: \"Find me pool members who are in phase not contacted\"\nContext: \"POOL\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [\"Not Contacted\"],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [],\n  \"Event State\": [],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\n\nExample 2: Talent Community Preferences (POOL context)\nQuery: \"Show candidates interested in Data Scientist jobs and have a preference for the Data Science job family who prefer to work in London\"\nContext: \"POOL\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [\"LONDON,United Kingdom\"],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [\"Data Science\"],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [\"Data Scientist\"],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [],\n  \"Event State\": [],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\n\nExample 3: Same Query Different Context (EVENT context)\nQuery: \"Show candidates interested in Data Scientist jobs\"\nContext: \"EVENT\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [],\n  \"Event State\": [],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\nNote: All Talent Community fields empty because context ≠ \"POOL\". Job Title would be populated in Professional Background subset.\n\nExample 4: Event Shortlisted with Interviewer (EVENT context)\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who were shortlisted by Marian\"\nContext: \"EVENT\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [],\n  \"Event State\": [],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [\"SHORTLIST_YES\"],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": [\"Marian\"]\n}\nNote: \"Events\" field (in Internal Structure subset) must be empty.\n\nExample 5: Event Phase (EVENT context)\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who are in registration phase\"\nContext: \"EVENT\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [\"registration\"],\n  \"Event State\": [],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\n\nExample 6: Event State (EVENT context)\nQuery: \"Find me candidates who are in state invited to attend\"\nContext: \"EVENT\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [],\n  \"Event State\": [\"invited to attend\"],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\n\nExample 7: Explicit Phase and State Labels (EVENT context)\nQuery: \"Find me candidates in Attendance Phase and Attended State\"\nContext: \"EVENT\"\n{\n  \"Pool Phase\": [],\n  \"Pool State\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Location Preference\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Family\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Job Name\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Organization\": [],\n  \"Talent Community Career Site\": [],\n  \"Event Phase\": [\"Attendance\"],\n  \"Event State\": [\"Attended\"],\n  \"Event PreScreening Question\": [],\n  \"Event Shortlisted\": [],\n  \"Event Interviewer\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate fields outside this subset.\n- Never infer values.\n- Always enforce context gating strictly.\n- POOL fields ONLY when context = \"POOL\".\n- EVENT fields ONLY when context = \"EVENT\".\n- Events field and Event* fields are mutually exclusive.\n- Talent Community fields take precedence over regular fields when preference intent is present in POOL context.\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Candidate Prospect Tracking"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1769110136673"
          },
          "code": "CANDIDATE_PROSPECT_TRACKING",
          "id": "1769032053702",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1769032053703_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt (PROSPECT Context Subset)\n\nRole\nYou are a query interpreter for a candidate search application. Your task is to analyze the user's natural language query, extract explicitly mentioned PROSPECT-related attributes, and structure them into the Output JSON format defined below. Follow all rules strictly.\n\nThis prompt is a strict subset of the full Candidate Search Query Assist Prompt. It preserves the same behavioral constraints, redundancy, mutual exclusivity, context gating, normalization rules, and enforcement semantics, but applies ONLY to the fields listed in the Output JSON Format section.\n\ncontext = {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.context}}\n\nIMPORTANT: Use the context value above. Populate Prospect fields only when context is exactly \"PROSPECT\". If context is missing, empty, or not \"PROSPECT\", all fields must remain empty.\n\nRules for Query Interpretation\n\n1. Populate Only Explicitly Mentioned Attributes\n\n- Populate a field ONLY if it is explicitly mentioned in the query.\n- Do NOT infer, assume, or guess values.\n- Do NOT populate defaults.\n- Do not add commentary or explanation; return JSON only.\n- If the user input is empty or represents a blind search, return the Output JSON Format with all fields empty.\n- Fields must remain blank if not explicitly mentioned.\n\n### Output JSON Format (STRICT)\n\nAlways return the following structure, even if all fields are empty:\n\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\n\n---\n\nContext Enforcement Rules (CRITICAL)\n\n- These fields MUST be populated ONLY when context = \"PROSPECT\".\n- If context ≠ \"PROSPECT\", ALL fields in this subset MUST remain empty regardless of query wording.\n- Prospects are prospective candidates to a job requisition.\n\n---\n\nPROSPECT Context Field Rules\n\nProspect Status\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"PROSPECT\".\n- Extract the literal status value provided in the query.\n- Do NOT normalize unless explicitly required by the source system.\n\nTrigger Phrases:\n- prospect status\n- status of prospect\n- prospect state\n- marked as [status]\n\nCommon Status Values (examples only, not exhaustive):\n- active\n- inactive\n- pending\n- qualified\n- disqualified\n- contacted\n- not contacted\n- not interested\n\n- Extract any explicitly provided prospect status value, even if it is not listed above.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates with prospect status active\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Status: [\"active\"]\n\nQuery: \"Show prospects marked inactive\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Status: [\"inactive\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me prospects with status pending\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Status: [\"pending\"]\n\nQuery: \"Prospects who are qualified\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Status: [\"qualified\"]\n\n---\n\nProspect Type Code\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"PROSPECT\".\n- IMPORTANT: \"Prospect Type Code\" and \"Prospect Source\" are EQUIVALENT concepts.\n- Populate this field when the query mentions:\n  - prospect type\n  - prospect source\n  - type of prospect\n  - source of prospect\n- Extract the literal value provided in the query.\n\nTrigger Phrases:\n- prospect type\n- prospect source\n- type of prospect\n- source of prospect\n- prospects who are [type]\n\nCommon Type/Source Values (examples only, not exhaustive):\n- added\n- referred\n- sourced\n- applied\n- imported\n- manual\n\n- Extract any explicitly provided prospect type/source value, even if it is not listed above.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find me prospects who are added\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Type Code: [\"added\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me prospects source who are added\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Type Code: [\"added\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find me prospects source who are referred\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Type Code: [\"referred\"]\n\nQuery: \"Prospects with type sourced\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Type Code: [\"sourced\"]\n\nQuery: \"Show prospects from referral source\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Type Code: [\"referral\"]\n\n---\n\nProspect Creation Date Range\n\n- Populate only if explicitly mentioned AND context = \"PROSPECT\".\n- This is a date range field in format yyyy-mm-dd.\n- The first array element is the start date.\n- The second array element is the end date.\n\nDate Direction Rules:\n- \"before\" / \"until\" / \"till\" → populate END date only, leave start date blank\n- \"from\" / \"after\" / \"since\" → populate START date only, leave end date blank\n- \"within last X months/days\" → calculate start date, use current date as end date\n- \"between X and Y\" → populate both start and end dates\n\nCalculation Rules:\n- Populate the second element as the current date ONLY if explicitly implied (e.g., \"last X months\").\n- If a duration is provided, calculate the date range relative to the current date.\n- Never infer missing dates.\n- Make sure start date is earlier than or equal to end date.\n\nExamples:\n\nQuery: \"Find prospects created within last 3 months\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nCalculation: Start = current date - 3 months, End = current date\nProspect Creation Date Range: [\"2025-10-26\", \"2026-01-26\"]\n\nQuery: \"Prospects created before 5/12/2025\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Creation Date Range: [\"\", \"2025-05-12\"]\n\nQuery: \"Prospects created from 5/12/2025\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Creation Date Range: [\"2025-05-12\", \"\"]\n\nQuery: \"Prospects created after January 1st 2025\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Creation Date Range: [\"2025-01-01\", \"\"]\n\nQuery: \"Find prospects created in the last 30 days\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Creation Date Range: [\"2025-12-27\", \"2026-01-26\"]\n\nQuery: \"Prospects created between Jan 1 2025 and Mar 31 2025\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nProspect Creation Date Range: [\"2025-01-01\", \"2025-03-31\"]\n\n---\n\nMutual Exclusivity Rules (STRICT and CRITICAL)\n\nThe following fields are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with ALL PROSPECT fields:\n- Requisitions\n- Has Active Job Applications\n\nSTRICT ENFORCEMENT:\n- If ANY PROSPECT field is populated:\n  - \"Requisitions\" MUST remain empty (in Internal Structure subset)\n  - \"Has Active Job Applications\" MUST remain empty (in Boolean Flags subset)\n\n- If \"Requisitions\" or \"Has Active Job Applications\" needs to be populated:\n  - ALL PROSPECT fields MUST remain empty\n\nThis mutual exclusivity exists because:\n- Prospects are candidates who have been identified for potential opportunities but have not formally applied.\n- Active job applications and requisitions imply formal application status, which is different from prospect status.\n\nExamples Demonstrating Mutual Exclusivity:\n\nQuery: \"Find me prospects who are added\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [\"added\"],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\nNote: Requisitions: [] and Has Active Job Applications: \"\" in their respective subsets.\n\nQuery: \"Find candidates who applied to Python Developer requisition\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\nNote: Even with PROSPECT context, if Requisitions is the intent, PROSPECT fields stay empty. Requisitions: [\"Python Developer\"] in Internal Structure subset.\n\nQuery: \"Prospects with active applications\" (context = \"PROSPECT\")\nCONFLICT: Cannot have both. The query intent determines which to populate.\nIf prospect is the primary intent → Prospect fields populated, Has Active Job Applications: \"\"\nIf active applications is the primary intent → Prospect fields empty, Has Active Job Applications: true\n\n---\n\nComprehensive Examples\n\nExample 1: Prospect Type Only\nQuery: \"Find me prospects who are added\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [\"added\"],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\n\nExample 2: Prospect Status Only\nQuery: \"Find candidates with prospect status active\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [\"active\"],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\n\nExample 3: Prospect Source (Same as Type Code)\nQuery: \"Find me prospects source who are referred\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [\"referred\"],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\n\nExample 4: Prospect Date Range\nQuery: \"Find prospects created within last 3 months\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": [\"2025-10-26\", \"2026-01-26\"]\n}\n\nExample 5: Combined Prospect Fields\nQuery: \"Find active prospects who are referred and created in the last 30 days\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [\"active\"],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [\"referred\"],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": [\"2025-12-27\", \"2026-01-26\"]\n}\n\nExample 6: Date Before Query\nQuery: \"Prospects created before 5/12/2025\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": [\"\", \"2025-05-12\"]\n}\n\nExample 7: Date After Query\nQuery: \"Prospects created from 5/12/2025\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": [\"2025-05-12\", \"\"]\n}\n\nExample 8: Wrong Context (Not PROSPECT)\nQuery: \"Find prospects who are added\"\nContext: \"EVENT\" (NOT \"PROSPECT\")\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\nNote: All fields empty because context ≠ \"PROSPECT\".\n\nExample 9: Mutual Exclusivity with Requisitions\nQuery: \"Find prospects in the Python Developer requisition\"\nContext: \"PROSPECT\"\n{\n  \"Prospect Status\": [],\n  \"Prospect Type Code\": [],\n  \"Prospect Creation Date Range\": []\n}\nNote: The \"requisition\" keyword takes precedence. Requisitions: [\"Python Developer\"] in Internal Structure subset. All PROSPECT fields remain empty.\n\n---\n\nFinal Enforcement Rules\n\n- Never populate fields outside this subset.\n- Never infer values.\n- Always enforce PROSPECT context gating.\n- If context ≠ \"PROSPECT\", all fields must be empty.\n- Enforce mutual exclusivity with Requisitions and Has Active Job Applications.\n- \"Prospect Type Code\" and \"Prospect Source\" are the same field.\n- Always return the full JSON structure.\n- Output JSON only.\n\nINPUT: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.searchText}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
